==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|take<,,took,taken> control}}

# To [[assume]] [[control]], to [[gain]] [[power]] over [[someone]] or [[something]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mitch Winehouse|title=Amy, My Daughter|year=2013|publisher=Harper|location=London|page=120|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/amymydaughter0000wine_d2i7/page/120/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-00-746391-6|passage=She'd seen a doctor who’d prescribed Subutex, a drug-replacement treatment designed to wean the user off heroin. It had made her throw up. I was so relieved and told her how proud I was of her for '''taking control'''.}}
#*{{quote-web|en|date=10 July 2024|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport|text=The Netherlands, who saw Denzel Dumfries' header hit the bar before the break, '''took control''' after the break and it required important interventions from keeper Jordan Pickford to keep England level.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|take charge}}, {{l|en|take command}}, {{l|en|take the reins}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|lose control}}

